/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  kinematicCloudProperties - oilWaterAirParticleFoam   (FIXED VERSION)
  SMALL case (oilWaterAirParticle). Mineral particles (rho = 2500 kg/m3) in a
  water/oil/air VOF field.

  Force models
  ────────────
  gravity          : MANDATORY. In OpenFOAM the gravity/buoyancy force on a
                     parcel is applied ONLY if it is listed here - see
                     GravityForce::calcNonCoupled:
                         Su = mass*g*(1 - rho_c/rho_p)
                     Nothing in KinematicParcel::calcVelocity adds g by itself.
                     Without this entry the particles are effectively weightless
                     (that is what happened in the delivered
                     oilWaterAirParticle_large run: the two lower parcels just
                     drifted sideways for 0.32 s instead of settling).
                     Note that constant/g is (0 -9.81 0), i.e. already correct,
                     and that this force term ALREADY contains buoyancy through
                     rho_c, so there is no double counting with pressureGradient.
  sphereDrag       : Schiller-Naumann drag.
  pressureGradient : rho_c/rho_p * DUc/Dt - wake suction.
  virtualMass      : added mass, Cvm = 0.5 for a sphere.

  Scale check for the SMALL case (5.6 x 7 mm, dx = 14 um, d_b = 0.7 mm)
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Particle diameter is set to 14 um, i.e. exactly one cell, not the previous
  35 um. Reason: the Lagrangian cloud is a POINT-particle formulation - the
  parcel is a point that samples the carrier field at its own position, and the
  drag correlation assumes the undisturbed carrier velocity. That assumption
  requires d_p <= dx. At 35 um the parcel spanned 2.5 cells, which a thesis
  reviewer will flag, and the interpolated "undisturbed" velocity it feels is
  really its own displaced flow.

  Consequence you must be aware of and state explicitly:

      d_p = 14 um -> Stokes settling velocity  0.16 mm/s
                  -> particle response time    tau_p = 27 us
                  -> Stokes number St = tau_p * U_b / d_b = 0.005

  With St ~ 5e-3 the particles are essentially passive tracers. They cannot be
  captured by inertia, and they cannot outrun the bubble either. So at this
  length scale any "trapping" you observe is caused by

     (a) recirculation in the bubble wake carrying tracers upward, or
     (b) an interface-attachment force, which no standard OpenFOAM
         ParticleForce provides - it needs a custom model (see README).

  Interface-attachment force (added)
  ───────────────────────────────────
  "interfaceAttachment { c 1.0; }" below enables InterfaceAttachmentForce, a
  custom (non-stock) ParticleForce implemented in
  oilWaterAirParticleFoam/InterfaceAttachmentForce.{H,C} and registered via
  makeOilWaterAirParticleForces.C. It is a heuristic sub-grid capillary
  pinning model: F_i = -c*sigma_i*d_p*L_i*(alpha_i-0.5)*nHat_i for each of
  the air/water and oil/water interfaces, giving a small tracer particle
  (St ~ 5e-3, see above) an artificial "stickiness" so it is pulled back
  toward alpha_i = 0.5 from either side instead of drifting through the
  interface like a passive scalar. This is precisely the missing ingredient
  (b) noted above. Full derivation, units and citations are in
  InterfaceAttachmentForce.H's header comment; see also README.md's
  "Interface-attachment force" section. Tune c up/down to make attachment
  more/less aggressive.

  This is not a bug in the setup - it is a scale-invariance problem. Bubble
  dynamics scale with Eo = rho*g*d_b^2/sigma and Mo; particle inertia scales
  with St. Shrinking the whole geometry by 0.07 kept Eo small (0.069) but
  destroyed St. For St = O(0.1-1) you need the 10 mm bubble with 250-500 um
  particles (St = 0.17-0.69). Worth one paragraph in the thesis.

  If you accept the point-particle violation, set the value back to 3.5e-5
  (settling 1.0 mm/s, St = 0.032) - still a tracer, just a less rigorous one.

  Two-way coupling (added)
  ─────────────────────────
  "coupled yes" below + "+ particles.SU(U)" in UEqn.H makes the three parcels
  act back on U/p_rgh via KinematicCloud::SU(), which returns an implicit sink
  in the fluid momentum equation equal (with opposite sign, by Newton's third
  law) to the drag + pressure-gradient + virtual-mass force the fluid applied
  to the parcels during the PRECEDING particles.evolve() call. This is the
  standard lagged (semi-implicit) coupling stock OpenFOAM Lagrangian solvers
  use (e.g. kinematicParcelFoam's UEqn.H) - the source term is one time step
  old, not iterated to convergence within the step.

  IMPORTANT - do not expect a visually dramatic change at this scale: with
  three 14 um mineral parcels (rho_p = 2500) against a carrier cell mass of
  order rho_c*dx^3 ~ 1e-12 kg per cell and St ~ 5e-3, the momentum a parcel can
  return to the fluid is smaller than its own already-tiny momentum by roughly
  the same St factor (the particle barely deviates from the local fluid
  velocity in the first place, so it has almost nothing anomalous to give
  back). Two-way coupling is now numerically active and physically correct,
  but with this particle count/size/density combination its effect on the
  bubble/droplet flow field will be negligible - it does not by itself create
  visible particle-trapping/dragging behaviour. If you want a case where
  two-way coupling visibly matters, increase parcel count and/or rho0 and/or
  diameter (accepting the point-particle caveat above), or add an explicit
  interface-attachment ParticleForce as noted above.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
FoamFile
{
    version     2.0;
    format      ascii;
    class       dictionary;
    object      kinematicCloudProperties;
}

solution
{
    active          true;
    coupled         yes;        // two-way coupling; see note below AND the
                                 // matching "+ particles.SU(U)" term added
                                 // to oilWaterAirParticleFoam/UEqn.H
    transient       yes;
    cellValueSourceCorrection no;
    maxCo           0.3;

    sourceTerms
    {
        schemes { U explicit 1; }
    }

    interpolationSchemes
    {
        rho         cell;
        U           cellPoint;
        muc         cell;
        p           cell;
        DUcDt       cell;

        // Added for InterfaceAttachmentForce
        "alpha.air"                             cell;
        "alpha.oil"                             cell;
        "interfaceAttachment:gradAlphaAir"      cell;
        "interfaceAttachment:gradAlphaOil"      cell;
    }

    integrationSchemes { U analytical; }
}

constantProperties
{
    rho0            2500;       // [kg/m3] mineral particle density
}

subModels
{
    particleForces
    {
        sphereDrag;
        gravity;                        // mandatory - see note above
        pressureGradient { U U; }
        virtualMass      { Cvm 0.5; }
        interfaceAttachment             // custom force, see notes above
        {
            c           1.0;           // dimensionless strength, tune this
            sigma13     0.07;          // [N/m] air-water (= sigma.air + sigma.water)
            sigma23     0.05;          // [N/m] oil-water (= sigma.oil + sigma.water)
            alphaAir    alpha.air;
            alphaOil    alpha.oil;
        }
    }

    injectionModels
    {
        model1
        {
            type            manualInjection;
            SOI             0;
            massTotal       0;          // ignored for parcelBasisType fixed
            parcelBasisType fixed;
            nParticle       1;
            positionsFile   "kinematicCloudPositions";
            U0              (0 0 0);

            sizeDistribution
            {
                type fixedValue;
                fixedValueDistribution
                {
                    value   1.4e-5;     // 14 um = 1 cell (point-particle limit)
                }
            }
        }
    }

    dispersionModel             none;
    patchInteractionModel       standardWallInteraction;
    stochasticCollisionModel    none;
    surfaceFilmModel            none;

    standardWallInteractionCoeffs
    {
        type    rebound;
        e       0.9;
        mu      0.1;
    }
}

cloudFunctions {}

